INTRODUCTION. 


The following genealogies have been prepared more particularly for the grat¬ 
ification and convenience of those directly concerned. As a whole, they may' 
serve the purpose of a general family register for reference, to the increasing 
posterity of those two venerable ancestors who have long since passed away. 
Samuel Packard and his numerous family were conversant with some of the Pil¬ 
grim Fathers of the Mayflower, and were a part of the Puritan stock by which 
the New England colonies were early settled. PCs posterity more or less have 
intermarried with the descendants of the immortal forty-one who signed the 
famous compact in the cabin of the Mayflower, in 1620. It is obvious from their 
record, that his posterity have, to a wide extent, been loyal and patriotic subjects 
of civil government, and stable supporters of Gospel Institutions. Large num¬ 
bers of them have belonged to the membership of Congregational churches. 

In the compilation of this work, the author would gratefully acknowledge the 
kind aid afforded him by numerous correspondents, and also his indebtedness to 
the History of Bridgewater, by Nahum Mitchell, in 1840, and the History of 
North Bridgewater, by Bradford Kingman, in 1867. 

i i So far as it can be ascertained, the Packards in the United States descended 
from Samuel Packard, who came from England to the Plymouth Colony eighteen 
years after the landing of the Pilgrims on Plymouth Bock. He came with his 
wife and one child, in the ship called the “ Diligent, of Ipswich, 133 passengers, 
•lohn Martin, master,” in 1638, and settled in Hingham, Mass. Settlements had 
been commenced in this place five years previously. He came from a small ham¬ 
let called Windham, near Hingham, which is about fourteen miles from Norwich, 
the capital of Norfolk County, in the eastern part of England: and it is some 
thirty-seven miles from Yarmouth, the seaport of the County, and about forty 
miles from Ipswich, the port from which he sailed, and one hundred miles north¬ 
east from London. 

' y Not far from 1660 Samuel Packard and his family removed from Hingham to 
that part of the original town of Bridgewater, Mass., which is now West Bridge¬ 
water. The original town of old Bridgewater was taken from Duxbury, and was 
deeded to Miles Standish, Constant Southworth, and Samuel Nash, on the 23d of 
March, 1649, by the Indian Chief, Ousamequin, who was the veritable Massasoit, 
and the grandfather of the famous Indian King Philip. The mark of 

Massasoit was affixed, and the deed is still extant. The articles paid for the land 
amounted to less than thirty dollars in value. The place had been called Nuckatest. 
Holmes’ American Annals say, “ The settlement of Bridgewater was begun in the 
5!ear 1651 by a very religious people.” Bev. James Keith, of Scotland, who was edu- 



