cated at Aberdeen, was ordained as the first minister of Bridgewater, Feb. 18, 
1664. The town was incorporated in 1656, and is said to have been named from 
an English Duke. The three towns, North Bridgewater, East Bridgewater, and 
West Bridgewater, have been taken from it. 

Samuel Packard was appointed to office in Bridgewater, in 1664, was licensed 
to keep an Ordinary in 1670, and his will was dated in 16iS4, and it is supposed 
that he died not long afterwards; probably between 70 and 80 years of age. 
Some of his sons, and probably he himself, were engaged as soldiers in the 
Indian war of King Philip, in 1675-6. They were among the twenty-one men 
who went out from Bridgewater and captured seventeen Indians and considerable 
plunder, on their way to meet Capt. Benjamin Church, under whose command, 
with an additional force of nine English soldiers and twenty friendly Indians, 
on the 2d of August, 1676, they killed or captured 173 Indians; and probably the 
same force, substantially, on the 12th of August, 1676, killed that famous Indian 
warrior at Mount Hope, slaughtered and scattered bis soldiers and ended the 
war—a war in which about six hundred of the inhabitants of New England were 
killed, twelve or thirteen towns entirely destroyed, and about six hundred build¬ 
ings burned. 

Six of the grandsons of Samuel Packard, and sons of Zaceheas Packard, viz.: 
David, Solomon, James, Zaecheus Jr., John, and Abiel Packard, removed to 
North Bridgewater not many years subsequent to 1700, and are the progenitors, 
probably, of the largest number of Packards in the country. 

The plan adopted in this work is to give a brief statistical account of each 
descendant in the regular order of generations, and of families. The genealogy 
of Samuel Packard, of Bridgewater, includes only the first four generations, and 
only those who have lived in Bridgewater, and the three towns that have been 
taken from it, viz.: East, West, and North Bridgewater. This brief and imper¬ 
fect account of 250 persons in four generations may, perhaps, aid in the future 
preparation of a more complete work brought down to the present time. 

The six generations of Abel Packard, of Cummington, who was the great- 
grandson of Samuel Packard, include all his posterity, living or deceased—352; 
Both genealogies include the descendants of these two ancestors, though they 
have by marriage merged the name of Packard in other names. 

The numerical arrangement indicates the order of the families in each gafcf 
eration, and of the children of each family. 

The star against names signifies deceased. The husbands and wives of t&ie; 
descendants are not enumerated among the posterity. 



