FIRST GENERATION 


1. * Abel Packard was the son of John Packard, the grand¬ 
son of Zaccheus Packard, and the great-grandson of Samuel 
Packard, who came from England to this country in 1638, and is 
the ancestor of the Packards in the United States. He was horn 
in North Bridgewater, Mass., Sept. 8, 1729. At the age of six¬ 
teen he was appointed chorister, and long led the choir in the 
Congregational church. He became blind at about forty years of 
age. In June, 1774, he removed with his widowed mother, wife 
and children, to what was then called No. 5; which was incorpo¬ 
rated June 23, 1779, and-called Cummington, in honor of Col. 
John Cummings, of Concord, who had purchased the township 
of the government, June 2d, 1762. His residence was a little 
west of the center of the town, and he kept a public house. 
The old u tavern sign ” is still preserved as a relic of the last 
century. The house which he erected in 1784, and the barn 
that was on the premises in 1774, are yet standing. The early 
town-meetings were held in his house. More or less he boarded 
the minister. He was an Ensign in militia, and tradition says he 
never knew what fear is. He married Esther Porter, of Abing- 
ton, Jan. 24, 1751. She was a descendant of the fifth generation,* 
it is supposed, of Nicholas Byram, who, “according to family 
tradition, was the son of an English gentleman of the county of 
Kent, England, who removed to Ireland about the time this son 
was born. His father sent him, at the age of sixteen, to visit his 
friends in England, in charge of a man who betrayed his trust, 



40 


GENEALOGY OF ABEL PACKARD. 


robbed him of his money, and sent him to the West Indies, 
where he was sold to service to nav his nassage; and after his 
term expired he made his way to New England and settled at 
Weymouth/' Ensign Abel Packard's brother, Barnabas, also 
removed to Cummington, and had seven children. Another 
brother, John, settled in Plainfield. His sister, Lydia, married, 
Edward Southworth, and settled in Pelham, and has a numerous 
posterity. Ensign Packard's cousin, Sarah Packard, married 
Esq. Ebenezer Snell, and settled in Cummington, and was the 
mother of Rev. Dr. Thomas Snell, and a grandmother of 
William Cullen Bryant, the poet, and of Prof. E. S. Snell, of 
Amherst College. In his immediate neighborhood in Cumming¬ 
ton, were raised the following persons known in public life, viz.: 
Hon. Luther Bradish, Hon. Henry L. Dawes, William Cullen 
Bryant, Rev. Dr. Thomas Snell, and Rev. Dr. Theophilus 
Packard. During the last century several descendants of the 
forefather, Samuel Packard, settled in Chesterfield, Pelham, 
Petersham, New Salem, Warwick, and Conway. Ensign Pack¬ 
ard's mother, Lidia (Thompson) Packard, lived with him in 
Cummington, and died there March 10, 1789, aged about 86. 
He had eleven children, all except the youngest of whom were 
born in North Bridgewater, viz.: Abel Jr., Esther, Adam, Lydia, 
Abigail, Mary, Olive, Theophilus, Jacob, Noah, and Abigail 2d. 
The father died in Cummington, March 4, 1804, aged 75. The 
mother died there, May 30, 1812, aged 79. As descendants of 
the Puritans, they honored their lineage by their Christian 
characters and lives. They had long professed the religion of 
the Gospel of Christ, and died in the membership of the Ortho¬ 
dox Congregational Church of Cummington. Well may their 
posterity respect and imitate such an ancestry. 


