GENEALOGY OF ABEL PACKARD. 


83 


Of Abel Packard's posterity 118 have deceased, and 234 are 
living. But few of them now reside in Cummington. They are 
living in various parts of Massachusetts, Connecticut, New 
York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Ohio, Michigan, Indiana, 
Illinois, Iowa, Wisconsin, Minnesota, Tennessee, Arkansas, Cali¬ 
fornia, and District of Columbia. Some of them are in business 
in Boston, New York, Cleveland, and Chicago. Ten- of them 
served in the army of the United States, in the war of the Re¬ 
bellion, viz.: James B. Carpenter, of Knoxville, Tenn.; Charles 
W. Dawes, of Cummington; Almarin S. Latham, Sidney H. 
Latham, Joseph Narramore, of Excelsior, Minn.; Francis W. 
Norton, of Princeton, Ill.; James Shaw Packard, of Brooklyn, 
N. Y., William B. Packard, of Five Comers, Miami Co., Ind.; 
William Henry Packard, of Pine Bluffs, Ark.; and Noah Sangs- 
ton Packard, who died in the army hospital at Nashville, Tenn. 

Several -of them have been Principals of Academies and High 
Schools, Editors and Authors, five Graduates of Colleges, viz.: 
Rev. T. Packard, D.D., Dartmouth, 1796; Rev. T. Packard, 
Amherst, 1823; Doctor Abel Packard, Amherst, 1824; Zalmon 
Richards, Williams, 1836; Prof. John L. T. Phillips, Williams, 
1847; one on the Board of Aldermen in Washington, and Su¬ 
perintendent of the Metropolitan Police in District of Columbia, 
viz.: Almarin C. Richards; two Principals of State Normal 
Schools, viz.: Fordyce Almon Allen, at Mansfield, Penn., and 
Charles Herman Allen, at Platteville, Wis.; one President of 
the New York Commercial College, and publisher of Packard's 
Monthly, viz.: Silas S. Packard; one a Professor in Williams' 
College, viz.: John L. T. Phillips; three Preachers of the 
Gospel, viz.: Rev. Dr. Packard, Rev. T. Packard, and Rev. 
Isaac Tirrill Packard, who died in Concord, 0., in 1849; three 
married ministers, viz.: Esther P. Williams married Rev. Alvah 
C. Page in 1835, Mary P. Richards married Rev. Burton B. 
Carpenter in 1841, and Sophia L. Porter married Rev. H. M. 
Smith in 1857; two lawyers, viz.: Horatio E. Porter and 
Samuel W. Packard; and one Doctor, viz.: Doct. Abel Packard. 



